  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Compute $P(X>1)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}0.1{e}^{-0.1x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & =P(1<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }0.1{e}^{-0.1x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-0.1x}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-0.1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
